{"query":"What does the claim mean by cutting the umbilical cord 'straight away,' and what timing thresholds (e.g., seconds, minutes) distinguish immediate from delayed cord clamping?","results":[{"position":1,"site_name":"www.whattoexpect.com","snippet":"Delayed cord clamping means waiting 30 seconds to three minutes after birth before cutting the umbilical cord, allowing more blood to flow from the placenta to the baby.","title":"Should You Delay Cutting Your Baby’s Umbilical Cord?","url":"https://www.whattoexpect.com/pregnancy/cut-umbilical-cord"},{"date":"Aug 9, 2023","position":2,"site_name":"www.who.int","snippet":"Early cord clamping is generally carried out in the first 60 seconds after birth, whereas later cord clamping is carried out more than one minute after the birth or when cord pulsation has ceased.","title":"Optimal timing of cord clamping for the prevention of iron ...","url":"https://www.who.int/tools/elena/interventions/cord-clamping"},{"position":3,"site_name":"scienceinsights.org","snippet":"The WHO defines early clamping as anything in the first 60 seconds, and late clamping as anything beyond one minute or when pulsation stops. In practice, many birth teams wait two to three minutes or simply let the cord go white and limp, which signals that the transfer is essentially complete.","title":"How Long Can You Wait to Cut the Umbilical Cord?","url":"https://scienceinsights.org/how-long-can-you-wait-to-cut-the-umbilical-cord/"},{"date":"Feb 20, 2025","position":4,"site_name":"www.ausmed.com","snippet":"Despite immediate clamping of the umbilical cord being standard practice until recent years, delayed cord clamping is now routine and is recommended for most births due to its numerous benefits.","title":"Umbilical Cord Clamping: How Long Should You Wait? - Ausmed","url":"https://www.ausmed.com/learn/articles/cutting-the-umbilical-cord"},{"position":5,"site_name":"idun.augsburg.edu","snippet":"One hypothesis behind this is that delayed clamping may increase the risk of the newborn developing polycythemia or hyperbilirubinemia or perhaps maternal postpartum hemorrhage. During the third stage of labor, the umbilical cord is normally clamped and then cut.","title":"Umbilical Cord Clamping: How Timing Affects Infant Health ...","url":"https://idun.augsburg.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2112&context=etd"},{"position":6,"site_name":"icea.org","snippet":"A 2012 Cochrane Review of early cord clamping versus delayed cord clamping or cord milking for preterm babies found that a delay of 180 seconds provided babies with additional blood through delayed cord clamping or milking the cord before clamping appeared to help the babies to adjust to their new surroundings with fewer transfusions for anemia ...","title":"Optimal Cord Clamping - icea.org","url":"https://icea.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/ICEA-Position-Paper-Optimal-Cord-Clamping.pdf"},{"position":7,"site_name":"snuggymom.com","snippet":"Immediate cord clamping (ICC) means cutting the cord within 15-30 seconds after birth. This practice was common for decades but has shifted with new research. Delayed cord clamping (DCC) refers to waiting at least 1 to 3 minutes before cutting.","title":"When To Cut Umbilical Cord? | Vital Timing Guide","url":"https://snuggymom.com/when-to-cut-umbilical-cord/"}],"total_results":7,"page":0}